    "body": "Problem:\r\nA Visual selection produced by a Lua mapping is captured as the keys it fed (`:norm!`), so non-primary cursors get the wrong selection.\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n- API cursor-moves around a fed \"o\" are lost.\r\n- `setpos(\"'<\")` + \"gv\" reselects the primary's marks (treesitter `an`).\r\n- A mapping that feeds an absolute selection (`:norm! <Esc>`, then `norm! 3GV6G`).\r\n\r\nSolution:\r\nClassify fed keys as `lossy` if their produced selection differs from the selection at mapping-end, or fed-key-start: the mapping moved it by other means (API). Same idea as `composite.lossy` LHS-replay.\r\n\r\nNow the captured Visual session contains the mapping's own cmd (`<Cmd>…`, `:…`, or a Lua mapping-id) as one subatom, instead of the keys it fed. E.g. `v…<K_LUA id>` instead of `v…V…G`.\r\n\r\nAlso affects dot-repeat (\".\").\r\n\r\nA `:norm!` that ends a typed selection keeps the Visual session (`vatom`) open (instead of the usual `atom_visual_reset()` plus emit/stage), so the enclosing user frame (the mapping's `<Cmd>`/Lua, or a typed `:norm!`) can complete or replace it.\r\n- A child frame re-entering Visual does not restart it, so a later `norm! V` continues that session instead of a new `kVatomFed` one.\r\n- At end of the user (toplevel) frame, the fed subatoms are dropped in favor of the mapping's own atom: as a subatom if Visual is active, else as the session's closing suffix.\r\n\r\nfix #41716\r\nfix #41956\r\nfix #41754\r\n",
